# CASP15 T1127 — The Plant's Chemical Weapon (8XBP)
---

Plants make an incredible arsenal of chemical
weapons to defend themselves.

This is AtNATA1 — an acetyltransferase from
Arabidopsis thaliana, the model plant
used in genetics research worldwide.

> close
> load 8xbp
---

AtNATA1 transfers an acetyl group from
acetyl-CoA to its targets.

This enzyme is part of the plant's defense
system — it modifies small molecules
to create compounds that are toxic
to herbivores and pathogens.

> spin 0.15
---

Here's the active site with acetyl-CoA bound —
the universal acetyl donor in biology.

The structure shows exactly how the enzyme
positions this cofactor to transfer
its acetyl group with precision.

> stop
> view ligand
---

Arabidopsis is to plant biology what
E. coli is to microbiology — the organism
we know best.

Understanding enzymes like AtNATA1 helps us
engineer crops with better natural defenses,
potentially reducing the need for
synthetic pesticides.

